The primitive everything else needs, and it has no command. Resolving a
number to a path was required to do almost anything by hand during 39's
audit, including by the audit's own commands:

  find .pidly -type d -name 17

And reading one ticket is that plus a cat:

  cat "$(find .pidly -type d -name 11)/info"

Two rows of the audit fold into the same traversal, because both are asking
for part of what a read would print: one field of one ticket, which is a sed,
and the field block, which is the awk the reference already publishes.

The shape worth considering is one command that prints a ticket and can print
less of it, rather than four commands. pidly field already prints one field
across every ticket; the missing direction is one ticket across its fields.

Careful with the path form: printing a path invites callers to build their
own idioms on it, which is fine — that is principle 1 — but a command that
prints a path must not become the thing agents use to hand-edit info files
that a command should be writing. See the sibling ticket on title and needs.

Done when a number resolves and a ticket reads without a find, the reference
keeps publishing the hand idiom beside the command, and the suite covers a
number that does not exist.


Done 2026-09-18. One command with flags, as decided: pidly field prints one
field across every ticket, and show is the other direction.

  pidly show 7              the whole info file
  pidly show -p 7           its directory
  pidly show -F 7           the field block
  pidly show -b 7           the body
  pidly show -f effort 7    one field's value

All four audit rows are the same traversal asking for more or less of it, so
they are one verb. -f is not checked against the schema, for the reason field
is not either: a ticket written by hand is a legal ticket and may carry a key
the schema never declared. An absent field prints nothing rather than failing.

-p prints an absolute path, so cat "$(pidly show -p 7)/info" works from any
directory. The ticket's worry about a resolved path inviting hand edits is
answered in the reference rather than by withholding the path: the table now
says a path is yours to read and not a licence to write lines a command owns,
and points at set and reparent.

The reference's read table gained a command column beside the idioms rather
than replacing them. The right-hand column is still what the file is, and
still works where pidly was never installed; the left is what to type when it
was. That is the property this ticket exists to keep.

Covered in views.sh: every mode, a number that does not exist, a field name
with a colon, -f with no name, and that a closed ticket still reads — show
answers about a ticket, not about what is takeable.
